														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1449438">
									

<p>This is&#8230; interesting. And it definitely has potential. But unless I'm misunderstanding something, anyone can pass on the story once they've been &quot;infected&quot; with it, right? Or is it only if they were told/exposed to the story originally from one of the tribe? I.e., would the exposed researcher be able to infect someone else with his story? Because if that's the case, this is clearly a Keter due to the INCREDIBLE difficulty in containing it. If the infectious story has to originate from a member of Tribe A, then it's <em>somewhat</em> easier to contain, and I agree that Euclid would be more appropriate.</p>
<p>Also, I can't tell: are the members of the tribe physically separated from each other in order to prevent them from telling each other the story? If not, why not? And why are we keeping them at the original tribal location? Why not move them to a more secure facility (again with the separation in private containment suites), and monitor the tribal location separately?</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>Giving bearhugs to the unsuspecting since 1872.</p>

